--- body ---
## Context

Two independent measurements have hit the same ceiling on upstream's JSON
(memory) search-index backend:

1. `bench/results/json-baseline/rung-rall-nochar.json` (2026-08-21, pre-v1.10.0
   session): the in-memory build reached the real library's full geometry
   (477 512 passages), but `search-index.json` could not be written — three
   occurrences of `WARN Could not persist index: Invalid string length` from
   item ~5 100 onward, leaving a stale 402 542-passage partial on disk.
2. `bench/results/trunk-1.10.0/build-memory-uncapped.json` (2026-08-30, this
   repo's reviewed baseline, b132f2d / v1.10.0): the same build, same
   geometry, a 10 GiB heap this time — the in-memory build completed in
   full, but persistence failed identically: repeated `WARN Could not persist
   index: Invalid string length` from item ~5 100 onward, final status
   `persistError: "Invalid string length"`.

Both hits are the same site and the same mechanism, confirmed by reading the
source at the reviewed SHA (`b132f2dcdf646424a236e5ede4ec68ab8a33adc6`,
v1.10.0):

- `fork/src/features/search/persistence.ts:27` —
  `await writeFile(tmp, JSON.stringify(index.toJSON()))`.
- `index.toJSON()` (`fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts:1676-1699`,
  `SearchIndexBase`) builds `{ chunks: [...this.chunks.values()], vectors:
  this.vectors.toJSON(), ... }` — every passage and every vector the index
  holds, materialized in one object, handed to one `JSON.stringify` call.

Upstream is already aware of the ceiling and documents it in three places at
this SHA: `persistence.ts:18-21`, `backend.ts:5-10`, and
`build.ts:116-121` (`persistNotice`, surfaced on `IndexBuildStatus`). The
documented mitigation is `ZOTEUS_INDEX_BACKEND=sqlite` (Node 22.13+), which
`factory.ts`'s `auto` mode already prefers when available
(`fork/src/features/search/factory.ts:52-56`). Both measurements above forced
the JSON/memory backend explicitly (`ZOTEUS_SEARCH_BACKEND=json` /
`--backend memory`) to characterize the ceiling itself; a default `auto` run
on Node 22.13+ would not hit this particular site. It remains reachable
whenever `ZOTEUS_INDEX_BACKEND=memory` is set explicitly, or `auto` falls
back on pre-22.13 Node.

## Site inventory (at b132f2dcdf646424a236e5ede4ec68ab8a33adc6, v1.10.0)

### Confirmed unbounded

1. **`fork/src/features/search/persistence.ts:27`** — `saveIndex()`,
   `JSON.stringify(index.toJSON())`. What accumulates: every `ChunkRecord`
   (all passages, all items) and every vector the in-memory index holds, in
   one V8 string. Not bounded: no cap exists between "index holds N
   passages" and "attempt to serialize all N in one string". Ceiling: V8's
   maximum string length, ~536 870 888 UTF-16 code units (~512 MiB) on
   current V8/Node; `JSON.stringify` can also throw a heap `RangeError`
   before that if the intermediate string construction exceeds available
   heap. Reached by our measured library (477 512 passages, 465 110
   fulltext passages, 44,9 MB max single document) at roughly 5 100-5 600 of
   7 541 items in both recorded runs — well inside the library's actual
   size, not at some hypothetical future scale.

2. **`fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts:572`** (`SearchIndexBase.build()`,
   shared by `MemorySearchIndex` and `SqliteSearchIndex` — both backends
   inherit it) — `this.opts.embedder.embed(records.map((r) => r.text))`.
   `records` accumulates over every item in the `libraryItems` argument with
   no batching at all, unlike the production incremental-build path (see
   bounded list below). If the configured embedder is `ApiEmbeddingProvider`
   (`fork/src/features/search/embeddings.ts:230-284`) constructed with no
   per-provider `batchSize`, `embedBatch()` performs exactly one
   `JSON.stringify({ model, input: texts })` (OpenAI,
   `embeddings.ts:260`) or one `requests: texts.map(...)` stringify (Gemini,
   `embeddings.ts:275-277`) of every text passed in — i.e. every passage's
   text from the whole `libraryItems` array, in one HTTP request body.
   **Reachability caveat**: `SearchIndex.build()` is part of the public
   interface (`backend.ts:436`) and is exercised only by the test suite (10
   test files call `.build(` directly) as of this SHA — the live MCP tool
   flow (`zotero_index action:"build"`) calls `buildIncremental` via
   `fork/src/features/search/build.ts`, which does batch (see bounded list).
   This is a real, currently-dead-in-production code path, not a live
   third hit — flagged because it sits on the same public interface as the
   confirmed persist-path defect and any future caller (or a change that
   rewires the tool to call `build()` directly) would reach it unbatched on
   both backends.

### Bounded by design (non-findings, with interactions noted)

- **`fork/src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts:566-579`** (`importJson`, the
  legacy-JSON-to-SQLite migration) reads a whole `search-index.json` into one
  string via `readFile(jsonPath, 'utf8')` before `JSON.parse` — same class,
  read side — but is guarded by `MAX_MIGRATION_BYTES = 200 * 1024 * 1024`
  (200 MB, `sqlite-index.ts:32`): above that size the file is left untouched
  and `storeNotice` reports why ("reading it needs roughly ten times its
  size in heap"), rather than attempting the read. This guard is itself
  evidence upstream has already reasoned about this class on the read side.

- **`fork/src/features/search/fulltext-source.ts`** `textFor()`
  (lines ~166-192) accumulates one item's attachment text into one string,
  capped by `ZOTEUS_INDEX_FULLTEXT_MAX_CHARS`
  (`DEFAULT_FULLTEXT_MAX_CHARS = 40_000`, `fulltext-source.ts:10`) — this is
  ticket 0024's I-3 (the 40k truncation cap vs R9), a design gap, not this
  class. The interaction: both measurements above set
  `ZOTEUS_INDEX_FULLTEXT_MAX_CHARS=0` (uncapped) specifically to reach the
  library's real fulltext geometry, and *that* is what makes the per-item
  text legitimately large (our library's max single document is 44,9 MB,
  within the task's own "one document in one string" carve-out) — but
  multiplied across 5 999 fulltext-bearing items and funneled through
  finding #1's single `JSON.stringify`, it is what makes the aggregate
  exceed V8's ceiling. Closing I-3 (a per-item cap) would not fix finding
  #1 (the aggregate has no cap of its own regardless of per-item size); the
  two are independent, compounding defects.

- **`fork/src/features/search/embeddings.ts:200-217`**
  (`LocalEmbeddingProvider.embed`) and the production embedding call sites —
  `index-manager.ts:1399` (`buildIncremental`'s pass), `index-manager.ts:1146`
  / `:1150` (its callers) — batch via `embedBatchSize` /
  `DEFAULT_EMBED_BATCH_SIZE` (`limits.ts`), the option documented at
  `backend.ts:249-250`. This is the live tool path and it is bounded by
  design; contrast with finding #2 above, which is the same embed-call shape
  with the batching removed.

- **`fork/src/auth/store.ts`** (OAuth token store: AES-256-GCM +
  base64, `Buffer.concat([iv, tag, ct]).toString('base64')` at line 189) —
  a single credential record, not library-scale.

- **`fork/src/lib/cimd.ts:116`** (`Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8')`,
  Client ID Metadata Document fetch) — capped by `maxBytes`, streamed rather
  than buffered without limit (see the file's own SSRF/size-cap comment
  block, lines 4-10).

- **`fork/src/features/fulltext/epub.ts`, `pdf-pages.ts`, `pdf-locate.ts`** —
  per-document string building (page/section `.join()`). In scope of the
  task's own carve-out: one document's text in one string is legitimate up
  to the library's measured ~45 MB single-document ceiling; these sites
  never concatenate across documents.

- **`fork/src/tools/tag-audit.ts`** `listAllTags` / `listItems` — page the
  whole library into in-memory *arrays* (not strings) of small objects, then
  feed a report bounded by the tool's own `limit` argument (default 50, max
  500) through `registry.ts`'s `ok()`, whose single `JSON.stringify` only
  serializes that bounded report. The unbounded accumulation here is an
  array, not a string passed through one stringify/parse/join/concat step —
  outside this ticket's class as scoped, noted for completeness rather than
  counted as a finding.

## Re-verification at v1.12.0 (2026-09-02)

Every site cited above was re-read at this repo's current reviewed baseline,
`b05ed69a88e3a0c1ef874f57f97a0e11ddf7ec3c` (v1.12.0, released 2026-08-31; see
`UPSTREAM`). The `fork/` working tree stands on a local branch
(`droplist-df-pruning`, 879b75b), not on the reviewed commit, so the blobs were
read by SHA rather than from the working tree.

**Verdict: both findings hold at v1.12.0.** Nothing upstream bounded either
site between v1.10.0 and v1.12.0. One finding got measurably worse, one count
moved, and most line numbers drifted.

### Per-site resolution

| Site (as cited at v1.10.0) | At v1.12.0 | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| `persistence.ts:27` `writeFile(tmp, JSON.stringify(index.toJSON()))` | `:27`, file unchanged | confirmed, no drift |
| `persistence.ts:18-21` (ceiling doc-comment) | `:18-21`, verbatim | confirmed, no drift |
| `index-manager.ts:1676-1699` `toJSON()` | `:2104-2129` | confirmed; body identical plus one new field (`if (this.library) snapshot.library = ...`, `:2127`). Still `chunks: [...this.chunks.values()]` and `vectors: this.vectors.toJSON()` in one object |
| `index-manager.ts:572` `SearchIndexBase.build()` unbatched `embed` | `:718` | confirmed unbatched: `await this.opts.embedder.embed(records.map((r) => r.text))`, no cap, no batch |
| `backend.ts:5-10` (StorageBackend V8-ceiling comment) | `:21-28` | confirmed, text verbatim; moved because `canonicalLibraryToken` was added above it |
| `backend.ts:249-250` (`embedBatchSize` option) | `:356-357` | confirmed, verbatim |
| `backend.ts:436` (`build()` on the public interface) | `:572` | confirmed, verbatim |
| `build.ts:116-121` (`persistNotice`) | `:131-137` | confirmed, text verbatim |
| `factory.ts:52-56` (`auto` prefers SQLite) | `:52-56`, file unchanged | confirmed, no drift |
| `sqlite-index.ts:32` `MAX_MIGRATION_BYTES` | `:33` | confirmed, still `200 * 1024 * 1024`; v1.12.0 adds a test-only override `opts.maxMigrationBytes` (`:131`, `:285`) — a seam, not a weakening |
| `sqlite-index.ts:566-579` `importJson` guard | `:892-917` | confirmed; guard now reads `bytes > this.maxMigrationBytes` (`:899`), same refusal and same `storeNotice` |
| `fulltext-source.ts:10` `DEFAULT_FULLTEXT_MAX_CHARS = 40_000` | `:11`, file unchanged | confirmed (citation was off by one at v1.10.0 too; `:10` is the closing `*/`) |
| `fulltext-source.ts` `textFor()` ~166-192 | `:167-194`, file unchanged | confirmed, per-item cap intact (`:173`, `:189`) |
| `embeddings.ts:200-217` `LocalEmbeddingProvider.embed` | `:251-268` | confirmed bounded, `batchSize ?? LocalEmbeddingProvider.BATCH_SIZE` |
| `embeddings.ts:230-284` `ApiEmbeddingProvider` | `:281-334` | confirmed; the mechanism finding #2 depends on is intact: `const size = Math.max(1, this.opts.batchSize ?? texts.length)` (`:296`) — no configured batch size still means one call carrying everything |
| `embeddings.ts:260` (OpenAI single stringify) | `:311` | confirmed, `JSON.stringify({ model: this.model, input: texts })` |
| `embeddings.ts:275-277` (Gemini `requests: texts.map`) | `:326-327` | confirmed, verbatim |
| `index-manager.ts:1399` (`embedPending`'s batched embed) | `:1745` | confirmed bounded, same `while (pending.length >= ...)` splice loop |
| `index-manager.ts:1146` / `:1150` (`buildIncremental`'s drain calls) | `:1052` / `:1066` | confirmed bounded; now routed through a local `embedPending(force)` closure (`:977-978`) over `embedBatchSize = opts.embedBatchSize ?? DEFAULT_EMBED_BATCH_SIZE` (`:852`) |
| `auth/store.ts:189` | `:189`, file unchanged | confirmed, single credential record |
| `lib/cimd.ts:116` | `:116`, file unchanged | confirmed, still streamed under `maxBytes` (the SSRF/size-cap comment is lines 5-11, not 4-10) |
| `tools/tag-audit.ts` `listAllTags` / `listItems` | `:23` / `:39`, file unchanged | confirmed, arrays not strings, report bounded by `limit` (`:70`, `:97`) |
| `fulltext/epub.ts`, `pdf-pages.ts`, `pdf-locate.ts` | `epub.ts:71` `sections.join('\n\n')`; the other two unchanged | confirmed per-document, never across documents |

### What changed between v1.10.0 and v1.12.0

1. **No bound was added on the JSON write side.** Grepping `src/` at v1.12.0
   for `Invalid string length`, `maxIndexBytes`, `MAX_PERSIST`, `estimateSize`
   and `string length` returns exactly one hit — the doc-comment at
   `backend.ts:26`. `saveIndex` still has no analogue of `importJson`'s
   `MAX_MIGRATION_BYTES` check. `limits.ts` gained 48 lines at v1.12.0, all of
   them full-text crawl concurrency (`DEFAULT_FULLTEXT_CONCURRENCY_LOCAL` /
   `_CLOUD`, `SATURATED_FULLTEXT_CONCURRENCY`), nothing on the persist path.

2. **Finding #1 is strictly worse at v1.12.0, by default.** v1.12.0 adds a
   third passage source: `own-words-source.ts` (+286 lines), child notes and
   PDF annotations. `ZOTEUS_INDEX_OWN_WORDS` is absent from `config.ts` at
   v1.10.0 and present at `config.ts:185` at v1.12.0 as `bool(true)` — **on by
   default**. `addOwnWords` (`index-manager.ts:1821-1837`) chunks each entry
   and calls `this.putPassage(rec)`, i.e. feeds the same `this.chunks` map that
   `toJSON()` materializes. So a stock v1.12.0 JSON-backend build now pushes
   metadata passages, full-text passages *and* own-words passages through the
   one `JSON.stringify`, and reaches the V8 ceiling at fewer items than the
   2026-08-21 and 2026-08-30 runs recorded. Not re-measured here — a build is
   a multi-hour job and the machine was busy; what is established is the
   mechanism and the default, both read at the SHA.

3. **Finding #2's reachability caveat holds; its count moved 10 → 11.** Test
   files calling `.build(`: 10 at v1.10.0, 11 at v1.12.0 (new:
   `tests/features/search-schema-migration.test.ts`). Production callers in
   `src/`: still **zero** — `git grep -F '.build(' <sha> -- src/` returns
   nothing, while the same grep for `.buildIncremental(` returns
   `src/features/search/build.ts:328`. The live tool path is unchanged.

4. **`build()` gained a dedup, not a bound.** v1.12.0 inserts
   `if (this.hasEmbedder && this.adoptVector(rec)) continue;`
   (`index-manager.ts:712`), the vector-salvage skip from #34. It can shrink
   `records` when a sidelined index already holds the same vector for the same
   text, but a first build adopts nothing and the embed call at `:718` is
   exactly as unbatched as at v1.10.0. `vector-salvage.ts` is SQLite-only and
   does not touch the JSON persist path.

5. **Citation nits, all pre-existing** (the files are unchanged, so these were
   already off at v1.10.0): `DEFAULT_FULLTEXT_MAX_CHARS` is at
   `fulltext-source.ts:11`; `textFor` is `:167-194`; `cimd.ts`'s SSRF comment
   block is `:5-11`. No mechanism is affected.

### Method

Blobs were read at both SHAs with `git show <sha>:<path>` from a read-only
`--shared` clone of the `fork/` object store; nothing under `fork/` was
written, built or run. Every negative result here carries a positive control
that came out the other way: the empty diffstat used to assert "file unchanged"
was run against `backend.ts` first, which returned `148 insertions(+), 4
deletions(-)`; the empty `.build(` grep over `src/` was paired with the same
grep for `.buildIncremental(`, which returned a hit; the absent
`ZOTEUS_INDEX_OWN_WORDS` at v1.10.0 was paired with `ZOTEUS_INDEX_BACKEND`,
which was present at `config.ts:175`.

## What an upstream fix would look like in shape

Not deciding for upstream — the design is his call — but the shape any fix
takes is constrained by what the measurements show:

- **Streamed/chunked persistence**: write `chunks` and `vectors` as a
  streamed sequence (e.g. newline-delimited JSON, or an array written
  incrementally with a stream-based JSON writer) instead of one
  `JSON.stringify` of the whole snapshot. Avoids the V8 string ceiling
  because no single string ever holds more than one record.
- **Per-item / per-batch persistence**: the SQLite backend already does this
  (rows, not one blob) — `ZOTEUS_INDEX_BACKEND=sqlite` is the existing
  answer for anyone who can move off the JSON backend. A shape-preserving
  fix for the JSON backend itself would need the same principle: persist
  in bounded units rather than one atomic whole-index write.
- **A guard on the write side symmetric with the read-side one**: `saveIndex`
  currently has no analogue of `importJson`'s `MAX_MIGRATION_BYTES` check —
  nothing estimates the serialized size before attempting the
  `JSON.stringify` and fails fast with a clear message instead of a bare
  `Invalid string length` three-quarters of the way through a multi-minute
  build. This would not fix the ceiling but would turn today's
  crash-late-and-lose-everything failure into a fail-fast one with a
  useful message, cheaply, on the existing backend.
- **Bound `SearchIndexBase.build()`'s embed call** (finding #2): batch it the
  same way `buildIncremental` already does, or delete the unbatched method
  if it is genuinely dead outside tests — either removes the latent
  unbounded-request shape from the public interface.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Findings #1 and #2 above are either accepted as accurate (no further
      investigation needed) or corrected, by a maintainer or a later session
      re-reading the cited lines at a current SHA: ACCEPTED AS ACCURATE.
      Re-read 2026-09-02 at b05ed69 (v1.12.0) — see "Re-verification at
      v1.12.0" above. Both findings hold, no bound was added on either site,
      and finding #1 got worse: own-words indexing is new in v1.12.0 and on by
      default, feeding a third passage source into the same unbounded
      JSON.stringify.
- [x] A decision is recorded on whether this repo files an upstream issue for
      finding #1: NOT filed — ruling 2026-08-30 (DECISIONS.md); the
      existing sqlite path is the sufficient upstream response.
- [x] Not filed upstream, so there is no issue to link — moot per the same
      ruling (was: link the issue here per `GOVERNANCE.md` if filed).
